course: language
lesson: 23
title: Borrowed words: French, Norse and Latin
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: English is a magpie: it picks up shiny words from every language it meets and keeps them.
explain: At dinner Mum says "We're having beef tonight", not "cow tonight"; after 1066 French-speaking lords ate the meat while English-speaking farmers looked after the animals, so the animal kept its English name and the meat got a French one.
explain: Before that the Vikings, who spoke Old Norse, had already given English everyday words like "sky", "egg", "window" and even "they".
explain: Latin, the old language of Rome and of European learning, keeps giving words too: "video", "audio", "a.m." and "bus" all come from it.
explain: The most common little words, like "the", "and", "you" and "water", are still mostly Old English; many of the long, fancy ones are borrowed.
fact: After 1066 English kept its animal words (cow, pig, sheep) but took French meat words: beef from "bœuf", pork from "porc", mutton from "mouton".
fact: "Sky", "skin", "skull" and "egg" came from Old Norse, the language of the Vikings, who settled in England from about 800 AD; a word starting with sk- is often Norse.
fact: "Shirt" and "skirt" began as the same old word: "shirt" is the English form and "skirt" the Norse form.
fact: "They", "them" and "their" came from Old Norse; borrowing such small everyday words is unusual and shows how closely the two peoples lived.
fact: The printer William Caxton told a story from the 1400s: a woman in the south did not understand a man asking for "eggys", but understood "eyren", the southern English word; in the end "eggs" won.
fact: "Window" comes from Old Norse "vindauga", meaning "wind eye", a hole in the wall for air and light.
fact: In Latin "video" means "I see", "audio" means "I hear" and "exit" means "he or she goes out".
fact: "Bus" is short for "omnibus", Latin for "for everyone", the name of big horse-drawn carriages for all passengers in the 1800s; cutting a long word short like this is called clipping.
fact: "a.m." is Latin "ante meridiem", before midday, and "p.m." is "post meridiem", after midday.
fact: "Mayday", the radio call for help, comes from French "m'aider", from "venez m'aider", "come and help me"; it was chosen in the 1920s.
fact: English took many food words from French, like menu, restaurant, café, croissant, baguette and chef; "chef" means "chief" or "head" in French.
fact: Studies of big dictionaries suggest that more than half of English words came from Latin and French, but the everyday core (the, and, is, you, house, water) is mostly Old English.
exercise: Ella asks why we say "beef tonight", not "cow tonight". What's the reason? | After 1066 French-speaking lords ate the meat and the French word stuck | Cows were already called "beef" in Old English, long before any French arrived | Beef is the name of the meat from a newer breed of cow brought over by the Vikings | Butchers made up the word in the 1900s
exercise: Which meat word comes from French "porc"? | Pork | Bacon | Ham | Sausage
exercise: Which group of words came from Old Norse, the language of the Vikings? | Sky, skin and egg | Shirt, shoe and ship | Menu, café and chef | Video, audio and exit
exercise: Which language gave English the word "they"? | Old Norse | French | Latin | Greek
exercise: In Caxton's story, why didn't the woman understand "eggys"? | In her part of England the word was "eyren" | She only spoke French, so no English word made sense to her | She was hard of hearing and couldn't catch what he said | "Eggys" was a type of bird
exercise: What does "window" mean if you look at its Norse parts? | Wind eye | Wind door | Glass hole | Light box
exercise: What does Latin "audio" mean? | I hear | I see | I sing | I go out
exercise: What is "bus" short for? | Omnibus | Business | Busy coach | Bustle
exercise: What does "omnibus" mean in Latin? | For everyone | Big wagon | Fast horse | Many wheels
exercise: What does "a.m." mean? | Before midday | After midnight | All morning | After midday
exercise: Where does "Mayday" come from? | French "m'aider", help me | The first day of May | An old English spring festival | A pilot called May
exercise: What does "chef" mean in French? | Chief or head | Cook | Kitchen | Knife
exercise: Jack says "English is 100 percent English words!" What's true? | More than half the words in big dictionaries came from Latin and French | He's right, English never borrowed a single word from any other language | Only one or two words in the whole of English are borrowed from other languages | Almost all words came from German
exercise: Which group of words is mostly Old English? | The, and, you, water | Menu, café, chef, croissant | Beef, pork, mutton, poultry | Video, audio, exit, omnibus
exercise: Shirt and skirt began as the same word. Which one is the Norse form? | Skirt | Shirt | Both are French | Neither, both are Latin
test: Your mate goes "the Vikings just smashed stuff, they never left any words, bruv." What's true? | English got everyday words like "sky", "egg" and "they" from them | He's right, not a single Viking word survived in English, they all vanished | They only left swear words, which is why we don't learn them at school | They only left names of weapons
test: Gran says "Mayday is a call about May Day, love, the spring holiday." Is she right? | No, it comes from French "m'aider", help me | Yes, it was first shouted on the first of May | Yes, it means "a nice day" | No, it comes from Latin "maximus"
test: Your Aussie cousin says "brekkie at 7 a.m., means after midnight, yeah?" ("Brekkie" means breakfast.) What does a.m. really mean? | Before midday | After midnight | Any minute | After midday
test: What would happen if no French words had entered English? | Words like "beef", "menu" and "chef" would be missing | English would have no words for any kind of food at all, not even "bread" | English would sound exactly the same, because French gave it nothing | English would be Latin
test: Leo reckons a printer in the 1400s made up the egg story to be funny. What does the story really show? | People in different parts of England once used different words | English was exactly the same everywhere in England back then, town by town | Nobody in England ate eggs in the 1400s, so nobody knew the word | Printers did not know English
test: A cartoon says "the word bus means BIG CAR in Old English!" What's the truth? | It's clipped from Latin "omnibus", for everyone | It's right, Old English had buses | It's from French "bus", meaning road | It's short for "business"
test: Mo's big sis says "video is a new word, Latin's dead so it can't give us words." What's fair? | Latin still gives English words, even for new technology | She's right, dead languages can never give any language new words | Video comes from Japanese, like "emoji" and "karaoke" | Video was invented by a TV company
test: Zara's mate from Glasgow says "aye, croissant's a pure Scottish word, ken." ("Ken" means you know.) What's true? | It's a French food word | It's Scottish from the 1900s | It's Norse | It's Latin for bread
test: Which of these is a clipping, like "bus" from "omnibus"? | Phone from telephone | Sky from Old Norse | Beef from bœuf | Window from vindauga
test: Why is it surprising that English borrowed "they" from the Vikings? | Small everyday words like that are rarely borrowed | Vikings never used the word "they" | "They" is a French word | English had no people in it
test: Your mate says "English is pure, mate, no foreign words." What's the best way to describe English? | A mixing bowl: an Old English core with lots of borrowed words | A pure language with no borrowings at all, just as it was 1,500 years ago | A dialect of French that lost its spelling rules after the year 1066 | A dialect of Latin
exam: Identify the principal reason for which English designates the animal "cow" but its flesh "beef". | Following 1066, French-speaking nobility consumed the meat while English-speaking labourers tended the livestock | Both terms derive from Old Norse, the former denoting the living beast and the latter its flesh when cooked | The term "beef" was devised by mediaeval butchers' guilds in order to distinguish their trade from that of farmers | The term "cow" is of Latin origin
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the word "they"? | It was borrowed from Old Norse, the language of the Viking settlers | It is of Latin origin and entered the language via the Church and its scholars | It was introduced by the Normans in 1066 together with words for law and food | It has existed unchanged in English since prehistory
exam: State the meaning of the abbreviation "p.m." pursuant to its Latin origin. | After midday | Before midday | After midnight | Past morning
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the composition of English vocabulary. | A majority of dictionary words derive from Latin and French, whereas the commonest words are chiefly Old English | English vocabulary is entirely of Old English origin | English vocabulary is entirely of French origin | Borrowed words are confined to scientific terminology
